The new variadic and ParamSpec resolution support has several edge cases that silently return incorrect type forms or mark valid defaults as unresolved. These affect advertised functionality around TypeVarTuple flattening, ParamSpec defaults, and Callable/Concatenate reconstruction.

Full review comments:

- [P2] Treat mixed TypeVarTuple empty markers as empty — /home/g/wt/paramsight/active/merge_reviewed_and_variadic/src/paramsight/_paramsight.py:321-324
  When an explicit empty `TypeVarTuple` appears alongside ordinary parameters, CPython leaves a `()` placeholder in `__args__` (for example `C[int, ()]`). This slice treats that placeholder as one absorbed argument, so `*Ts` becomes `((),)`; a base like `Callable[[*Ts], R]` then resolves to `Callable[[()], R]` instead of the intended no-argument `Callable[[], R]`. Detect this empty marker before building `absorbed`.

- [P2] Coerce ParamSpec parameter-list members — /home/g/wt/paramsight/active/merge_reviewed_and_variadic/src/paramsight/_paramsight.py:178-180
  ParamSpec parameter lists can arrive with raw source-form members such as `None` or strings, and Python does not coerce those inside `C[[...]]` the way ordinary type parameters are coerced. This means `class C[**P](B[Callable[P, R]])` resolves `C[[None, "Foo"]]` as `Callable[[None, 'Foo'], R]` instead of `Callable[[NoneType, ForwardRef('Foo')], R]`, leaking non-type values into the resolved form. Coerce each member before substituting it.

- [P2] Normalize ParamSpec defaults as parameter lists — /home/g/wt/paramsight/active/merge_reviewed_and_variadic/src/paramsight/_paramsight.py:290-290
  When an unfilled ParamSpec has a PEP 696 list default, this ordinary default path sends the list to `_substitute_typevars`, which returns lists unchanged. For `class C[T=int, **P=[T]](B[Callable[P, R]])`, bare `C` leaves `P` as `[T]` and reports the callable unresolved instead of substituting the earlier default to `Callable[[int], R]`. Route ParamSpec defaults through the parameter-list normalization path.

- [P2] Preserve Concatenate prefixes for ellipsis tails — /home/g/wt/paramsight/active/merge_reviewed_and_variadic/src/paramsight/_paramsight.py:196-197
  For `Callable[Concatenate[int, P], R]` with `P` specialized as `...`, this returns a bare `Ellipsis`, so the caller rebuilds `Callable[..., R]` and drops the required leading `int`. `Concatenate[int, ...]` is a valid runtime form, so this should preserve the prefix whenever `new_prefix` is non-empty.

- [P2] Flatten starred tuple aliases during substitution — /home/g/wt/paramsight/active/merge_reviewed_and_variadic/src/paramsight/_paramsight.py:157-166
  Python represents `*tuple[T, str]` as an unpacked `types.GenericAlias`, not as `typing.Unpack`, so `_is_unpack(a)` is false here and the substituted tuple is appended as a single argument. A base like `B[tuple[*tuple[T, str]]]` therefore resolves under `T=int` to `tuple[tuple[int, str]]` instead of `tuple[int, str]`. Handle `__unpacked__` tuple aliases in this flattening path.
